{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris","id":1485250,"title":"Haired Children of Osiris!?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-28T20:21:55-07:00","id":1485250,"title":"Haired Children of Osiris!?","content":"So, I've never allowed anyone to play a Children of Osiris in my games. I'm far from an expert on the bloodline for this very reason (also, there's not much material about them to read in any case...). So, my question is, while Children of Osiris are always described to shave their heads, does it ALWAYS HAVE TO BE the case? I mean, I have a girl from one of my tables and she wants to make a Children of Osiris as her next character (because the current is probably going to be destroyed), but she said she really couldn't stand to play a &quot;bald girl&quot; and asked me if she can make a converted child of Osiris with normal hair. So, what would you do in my position? Do you think it's ok to allow it? This bloodline has always struck me as &quot;weird&quot;, but I want to experience something new...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T21:20:18-07:00","id":1485253,"title":"","content":"I don't remember any statement being made that the Children of Osiris shaved their heads. But I could easily have overlooked that. Do you have the source?<br /> <br /> Now I've seen at least one picture showing one of the Children with a shaved head, but I think that was to illustrate someone who had been an actual ancient Egyptian, and the ancient male Egyptians shaved their heads (the women didn't).<br /> <br /> I also don't think it makes much sense. Although there is probably a core of ancient Egyptian Methusalehs among the Children of Osiris, I would imagine most of their membership now were of vampires recruited afterwards. They would have whatever hair they had at the time of the embrace. And if they shave it afterwards, it would keep coming back. Not much purpose in shaving it day after day - that has nothing to do with the real reason the Children of Osiris exists.<br /> <br /> So I think it's totally fine for them to have hair.<br /> <br /> Also, the Children of Osiris aren't actually a bloodline. They are a sect with access to a unique Discipline, and its members don't embrace. Sometimes the sourcebooks forget this.<br /> <br /> BTW, you may be interested in this <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1450257-children-of-osiris\">earlier forum discussion on the Children of Osiris, and their comparison to the Salubri</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T21:51:40-07:00","id":1485255,"title":"","content":"The shaving of the heads in ancient Egypt was due to lice problems, so both genders did it, those of standing wore wigs rather than simple head wraps or some form of cowl.<br /> <br /> As for the Children of Osiris being bald, at least in V20...<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Appearance: Since the Children can hail from any Clan or bloodline, their appearance can vary widely. They all shave their heads every evening upon awakening, and when in their temples they wear white robes adorned with mystical symbols and hieroglyphs. When leaving their temples on business, they dress simply and stay inconspicuous.&quot; </div> </div> </div>At a guess, the nightly head shaving is part of the self-discipline and dedication to the unlife style","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T22:44:21-07:00","id":1485260,"title":"","content":"Unless there a reason NOT shaving her head would make her overpowered, I'd just throw the shaved head thing out the window. <br /> <br /> Then again, some chicks rock the chrome dome rather well...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3201-father-enoch","name":"Father Enoch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3201_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"285"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T23:18:00-07:00","id":1485263,"title":"","content":"The Children of Osiris are essentially a monastic order, and I can understand them subjecting themselves to some kind of Rule. But the idea that they must shave their heads seems to me some kind of affectation, and if the V20 quote from <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar727_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"727\" data-vbnamecard=\"727\">Thoth</a> is its origin, then it is likely just some flavor text a late author decided to add. It is not something mentioned in the description of the Children of Osiris in 1992's The Hunters Hunted. If you like it, keep it. If you don't, ignore it.<br /> <br /> Even if you want to keep that as some kind of monastic discipline when the member of the Children is at temple, it's a rule likely to be relaxed when that person is sent outside the temple to do whatever work the local temple wants them to do. You don't want your undercover operatives to be noticeable when they are on a mission.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-29T06:22:18-07:00","id":1485280,"title":"","content":"From what I read, I really agree with most here, I don't see a reason for them to be forced to do it, also, Kemintiri is said to have used Bardo to keep her humanity and she doesn't strike me as someone that would follow their &quot;discipline&quot;, so to speak, in the sense that she's far from a &quot;human being&quot;. What strikes me as weird on the Children of Osiris is the fact that their powers are tied to Humanity and these &quot;ritual things&quot;. I mean the girl even asked me if she would lose her powers if she cut her hair lol and the worse part is that I didn't know how to proper answer the question (because of the aforementioned thing that ties their power to humanity), but I answered that it wouldn't make sense for her to lose her powers just because she isn't following the rituals of the order, but then again it all feels so strange that makes me confused. The thing is she just wants to have her hair for aesthetical reasons, no power involved (she's the typical &quot;goth girl&quot;, so it's no wonder she can't see herself without her long hair, even if it's just a character lol). I told her I needed some time to considerate, among other things, but I'm really inclined from the start to allow her to go ahead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-29T07:30:14-07:00","id":1485288,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I'd probably say that like, if she goes to temple for some sort of formal function (like a full on ritual in front of other members, not just showing up and giving a report to a higher up and then leaving) she'd be expected to shave her head like everyone else there. Many religious orders ask monks to shave their heads. But away from the temple she could do whatever she wanted. Since her character's hair regrows every night, and assuming she doesn't end up spending much time in a temple, her character might be bald for all of one or maybe two scenes across the entire campaign which probably wouldn't be a deal breaker.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-29T14:44:17-07:00","id":1485314,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485255#post1485255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The shaving of the heads in ancient Egypt was due to lice problems, so both genders did it, those of standing wore wigs rather than simple head wraps or some form of cowl.<br /> <br /> As for the Children of Osiris being bald, at least in V20...<br /> <br /> <br /> At a guess, the nightly head shaving is part of the self-discipline and dedication to the unlife style</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Thanks! The discussion is very interesting indeed. By the way, do people know any cannon Children of Osiris in the by nights books? I don't remember seeing any.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-29T17:16:38-07:00","id":1485321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485314#post1485314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Thanks! The discussion is very interesting indeed. By the way, do people know any cannon Children of Osiris in the by nights books? I don't remember seeing any.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Nope, truth be told the Children have seen little to no expansion or update since they were presented in the original Hunter's Hunted (what might might be at least in part the problem with Bardo as a discipline, in fact).<br /> <br /> The closest to an actual Child of Osiris one might get anywhere are Kethamon, who gets name-dropped half a dozen times in the background of Kemintiri or some other major OP NPCs, Samal Ramal, a setite (!) archon in Berlin by Night or Milarepa, a 5th gen tibetan monk (and possible source of the Bardo name for their discipline) mentioned in Caerns: Places of Power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 06-29-2022, 08:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T07:56:08-07:00","id":1485691,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485280#post1485280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kemintiri is said to have used Bardo to keep her humanity and she doesn't strike me as someone that would follow their &quot;discipline&quot;, so to speak, in the sense that she's far from a &quot;human being&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>She's ex-Children, that's why she knows and uses Bardo.<br /> <br /> \u200bAnd as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar727_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"727\" data-vbnamecard=\"727\">Thoth</a> said it is commonly thought that Ancient Egyptians shaved regardless. This is not actually true, it depended on a lot of context and we're still learning the details. Even finding actual scientific papers on the matter is hard, and outside it this is really taken as fact.<br /> <br /> But with the trope in place, and considering Kemintiri's situations anyways, I think she's probably bald, using either wigs or <b>Obfuscate</b> to make up for it. She was a priestess, and that makes for a higher probability of hair either shaved or at least wore short in Egypt (many sources depict shorter hair in religious contexts and many indicate that priests probably shaved, at least men, but maybe women), and if that wasn't the case, then Sutekh would taken care of this just to piss her off (she was a priestess of Isis, Embraced by him out of spite and vengeance because she didn't revealed the Spell of Life).<br /> <br /> I decided to confirm things in their original write-up in Hunters Hunted and it is actually there: page 51, title <i>Methods</i>, second paragraph.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hunters Hunted pp 51</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Appearance: </b>Their physical traits vary, as they recruit<br /> from various other clans, including Nosferatu. They are<br /> usually adorned in pure white robes, sometimes trimmed<br /> with mystical symbols and mandalas. They never wear items<br /> that would distract them from their all-important discipline,<br /> like the secular distractions of T-shirts or expensive clothing.<br /> <b>They are all shaved bald, including females</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, as others pointed, I don't think either the extreme keeping of the description is strictly needed or that they would demand such from members in a mission outside the temple.<br /> <br /> Strictly speaking, they're monks. To be precise, they're Nepalese/Tibetan monks despite the Egyptian background. From there you can read the Children in a number of ways depending how you want to depict the order. If you want them more open and trying to interact with Kindred society at large, then it isn't unfeasible to have &quot;lay followers&quot; that do not live in temples, do not follow the monastic rules, and it is on you if they would teach <b>Bardo</b> to such people or not, but it is actually not unlikely. Kemintiri herself never caused any trouble for them from knowing it, by their description it is less a secret and more something hard to keep and use without rigid discipline and their monastic life.<br /> <br /> Just talk to the player to see what she knows and what she expects from the character, not only for the bald thing, but for their whole outlook. Even if a Children Embraces, the childer won't be members until they get into it by themselves. Their standard depiction isn't in HH for nothing, they're monks that leave the monasteries for only three reasons: maintenance needs of the temple and its inhabitants, hunting FoS and inviting new members from vampires that seem to keep consistently at Humanity 8. But since the sect has so little written about it since 1992, you can stretch things a lot to accommodate your needs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":".  typos 07-03-2022, 04:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T09:52:15-07:00","id":1485699,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> She's ex-Children, that's why she knows and uses Bardo </div> </div> </div>Yes, sure. What scares me is that she had Bardo 7 (!!!). So, she's basically an exception to the rule of keeping humanity otherwise losing levels of Bardo, makes one wonder if that theory some believe is true, that is, that the Children are her pawns. It's a pity we don't have any Children character in any of the official settings' &quot;by nights&quot; books or chronicles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T10:51:31-07:00","id":1485703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485691#post1485691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I decided to confirm things in their original write-up in Hunters Hunted and it is actually there: page 51, title <i>Methods</i>, second paragraph.</div> </div> </div> </div>I must have read past that passage a dozen times and never noticed it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485691#post1485691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Strictly speaking, they're monks. To be precise, they're Nepalese/Tibetan monks despite the Egyptian background.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're definitely vampire monks. And since they want to blend it without anyone noticing them as something different, it behooves them to be indistinguishable from any other Buddhist monks found in the area. But it's important to note that the Children of Osiris are based in India because their founder Khetamon fled there after he helped Isis resurrect Osiris more than 5000 years ago. So he predates any tradition of Himalayan monasticism.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T13:38:17-07:00","id":1485714,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485699#post1485699\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, sure. What scares me is that she had Bardo 7 (!!!). So, she's basically an exception to the rule of keeping humanity otherwise losing levels of Bardo, makes one wonder if that theory some believe is true, that is, that the Children are her pawns. It's a pity we don't have any Children character in any of the official settings' &quot;by nights&quot; books or chronicles.</div> </div> </div> </div>With our entire source on the Children being HH and her own write-up, you can make whatever from this, up to and including a retcon that deals away with this rule for them as much as for her. Which I would do, as I think that just making the power inaccessible is better than wiping it from the sheet, which is kinda stupid.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485703#post1485703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But it's important to note that the Children of Osiris are based in India because their founder Khetamon fled there after he helped Isis resurrect Osiris more than 5000 years ago.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, definitely, I'm aware of that, but my point was about trope/doylist perspective.<br /> <br /> While it makes very little sense for them to be Tibetan monks, they're stereotypical (and I mean US stereotype) Tibetan monks, with a whiff of Nepal in there (because I guess most people in the Americas don't know the difference). Everything about their depiction is far more stereotyped Tibetan Buddhism than anything else, up to and including the fact that it had a huge influence from early Vajrayana from northern Medieval India, so their story kind of hints to the Children being the true bringers of this influence to Tibet. Despite making their main temple in Nepal because reasons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T16:08:28-07:00","id":1485727,"title":"","content":"I mean, having the Children of Osiris shave every day just to make themselves even MORE likely to be targeted by the Followers of Set and easily identifiable is incredibly stupid.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T17:57:43-07:00","id":1485734,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485727#post1485727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, having the Children of Osiris shave every day just to make themselves even MORE likely to be targeted by the Followers of Set and easily identifiable is incredibly stupid.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily.<br /> <br /> First, they look like Buddhist monks. What do you expect a vampire that was a Buddhist monk in life to look like? Or what do you expect from a vampire that converted after death and became a monk? The Children do not hide that they're monks, they hide the nature of their order if that much.<br /> <br /> Second, as far as we know the FoS have no clue that the Children still exists. The order is small, tightly united and lives in secluded areas far away from anywhere the FoS have any presence. It's easy to see the signs when you already knows the answer, but what is really the most probable cause of a random vampire being bald? Even being a FoS from another cell is far more likely than being a CoO <u>even for those who know about them</u>, imagine for someone who never heard about them (it isn't likely that the FoS tell this every new Embrace just 'cause) or fully expect them to have been wiped out thousands of years ago.<br /> <br /> Third, and compounding to the above, the FoS think they wiped the Children before the CoO went to north India, so current depiction has zero to do with the CoO as the FoS knew them.<br /> <br /> And fourth, depending on your depiction of the Children, it simply doesn't matter. They seldom appear on the world and then they go quickly and directly through their businesses before going back to their temples. They have no greater stakes, much less participation, on local politics except on matters that relate to the temple. They're not on the Jyhad, their's is not a hidden war. If you go through this route, while it is certainly risky to not make your presence a secret, it is just another approach to your conflict. One very similar to how other Hunters behave.<br /> <br /> Remember, the Children of Osiris are first and foremost defined in the game as Hunters, as a Hunter trope. That changes their role and their uses in the setting drastically. While it doesn't mean they can't work in a game with other vampires, even with Followers of Set, as they're not necessarily zealots and even <b>have</b> FoS among their numbers, it does mean they approach the whole thing from a place very different from other vampires and more closely related to some human Hunters with just as much high Humanity.<br /> <br /> Of course, those things do not add up well without making more stuff about them, but that comes with the territory of working with a group that was only ever described briefly in a single supplement where they were barely more than a side note. They're not a finished product in comparison with almost everything else in the setting, in fact many of the other groups in that same book got more space in later products than them. But they definitely can be fitted to work just fine as in-the-open opposition that denounces the FoS at every opportunity and sometimes come to blows with them without hiding their identities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T19:05:42-07:00","id":1485736,"title":"","content":"Player knowledge is often far greater than character knowledge. Just imagine the difference that would happen in a game where the players only knew the information in the 2e core rulebook in a chronicle that utilized concepts from Elysium, Storyteller Guides, the Players Guide and Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand versus players who had read all those books. The second group of players already know all the secret information that are supposed to be mysteries. They are going to be able to recognize certain NPCs as likely to be members of the Salubri, True Brujah, Children of Osiris, one of the Gehenna Cults, the True Black Hand, and so on. The first group will be perfectly oblivious. All they would know is that there is some stranger not getting very excited at a Brujah Rant, someone who's been observing them who had shaving equipment in a bag once they track him down to an apartment, a child who seems to be the head of some cult, and so on. It's going to take them a long time before they figure out anything if ever.<br /> <br /> I remember in one LARP when I had my character constantly refer to the Sabbat as the &quot;Black Hand&quot; as its written in the corebook as just a synonym for them. I constantly had certain players interrupt the game to point out my character couldn't know about that sect inside the Sabbat or what they did and so on, and I had to remind them that to most vampires, it's just a synonym for the Sabbat. After looking at me blank faced for a while, they then went back in character and then had their character laugh at my character for being so ignorant that I didn't know all this secret information that their characters shouldn't know either.<br /> <br /> It is just what happens when well read players likely know more than 90% of all vampires in the setting. It's hard to keep a sense of mystery if you just stick with canon. That's why I change a lot of the background material to retain a sense of mystery and name drop stuff that doesn't exist. Many players already know the Salubri, Tal'Mahe'Ra, Children of Osiris, and so on. And even the best roleplayers who try to excise OOC knowledge from their characters can't help but ruin the mystery. But if I name drop the Brotherhood of Enoch of instead, or the Children of the Nephilim, or Disciples of Zagreus, they have no idea of what I'm talking about and can't metagame.<br /> <br /> I think the problem is less that the Setites would know the Children of Osiris exist, or know they have a monastic rule of shaving their heads, and therefore able to identify them by such methods (too bad for any actual vampires who do happen to be bald naturally by the time they are embraced), the real issue is whether they draw attention to themselves. Most bald women are going to attract attention regardless of where they are. Therefore, it makes the most sense that when the Children of Osiris send agents into the outside world, they issue a dispensation that they do not have to follow most of their monastic rules. What's important is completing their mission, whatever that is, in secret. When they return to the temple, then dispensation ends and the rules go back into effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-03T23:59:06-07:00","id":1485749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris?p=1485736#post1485736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the real issue is whether they draw attention to themselves. Most bald women are going to attract attention regardless of where they are. Therefore, it makes the most sense that when the Children of Osiris send agents into the outside world, they issue a dispensation that they do not have to follow most of their monastic rules. What's important is completing their mission, whatever that is, in secret. When they return to the temple, then dispensation ends and the rules go back into effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mostly agree, I would just point out that depending on the actual mission and how it will be carried, this dispense wouldn't be the same. Again, if you pose as an actual Buddhist you mostly have a free pass to keep going as a Children, except maybe the color of your clothes (and even then that depends, some monks do use white instead of the more common red and yellow). If you infiltrate punk culture, some women do shave their heads there. Your mission may also not involve being subtle.<br /> <br /> Again, while I do agree that giving a pass is perfectly reasonable and don't even need that much justification (you're not in the temple, you're on a mission, you're blowing your discipline and monastic routine anyway, hair just isn't important), it is just to also point out that a player that for any reason wanted the opposite shouldn't be punished either: there are too many more likely reasons for a woman to be bald or shave.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-04T00:36:49-07:00","id":1485751,"title":"","content":"The main reason Egyptians in ancient times were thought shave their head was likely to because of lice infestations. This did eventually makes it way into becoming common practice for priests of the area and time, which is likely were the inspiration for this idea for the Children of Osiris'.<br /> <br /> That said, I vote making it traditional, but not required.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><div align=\"left\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/7.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26764-vilenecromancer","name":"Vilenecromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26764_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"247"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-04T07:24:28-07:00","id":1485771,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1485250-haired-children-of-osiris#post1485250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I've never allowed anyone to play a Children of Osiris in my games. I'm far from an expert on the bloodline for this very reason (also, there's not much material about them to read in any case...). So, my question is, while Children of Osiris are always described to shave their heads, does it ALWAYS HAVE TO BE the case? I mean, I have a girl from one of my tables and she wants to make a Children of Osiris as her next character (because the current is probably going to be destroyed), but she said she really couldn't stand to play a &quot;bald girl&quot; and asked me if she can make a converted child of Osiris with normal hair. So, what would you do in my position? Do you think it's ok to allow it? This bloodline has always struck me as &quot;weird&quot;, but I want to experience something new...</div> </div> </div> </div>CoO are not fanaticals; while they are monks, and requires certain didcipline, they are not bound by meaninless trivia. If the vampire were to express her desire to keep her hair, they would be accomodating, althought shunning her a little for it, but as long as it didnt became a distraction, it would be fine. And if she dedicates herself to the cause, she'll be exalted despite that.<br /> <br /> Oh, I once played a Children of Osiris... Who were a former Follower of Set, on top of it all. In a game of &quot;the Second City&quot;, where we were all 4th gens - so yes, I was basically a different version of Kemintiri, althought she were older than me. I met her, by the way. Set took interest on me because my back story was about me being a former exiled prince from another land, who had been denied my birthright to be a king... My own family disoned me in favor of my sister, something about me being a &quot;tyrant&quot; or something, so I had to flee for my life, and thus I decided to seek the &quot;city of warlocks&quot; (the lands of Nod), to try to &quot;persuade&quot; the &quot;warlocks&quot; that lived in there to help me seize back my crown and get revenge... So, once I got to the city (with pockets filled with gold), I started digging around... I discovered the structure of &quot;clans&quot; that the &quot;warlocks&quot; had - because as my ST described (a great ST by the way), they operated in the open, so I did my research, and decided that the ideal clan to help me would be the Lasombra... I've been captured however, slight tortured and mind graped without the g by Set himself, and had to reveal my true purpose... And when he figured it out that my purpose was revenge, he got all excited. And ended up ruining my plans to join the Lasombra.<br /> <br /> Anyway, he turned me into a ghoul, but I... managed to &quot;embrace myself&quot; with some help, using Set's blood and some help.<br /> <br /> Afterwards, I wanted to go right back home, but Set decided to &quot;indoctrinate&quot; me first. It... Wasnt what I wanted. My character was a would-be-tyrant, but that's because he believed himself to be officially &quot;da best&quot;, and in his mind he wanted to rule &quot;for the greater good&quot; (just like ALL tyrants), however what Set showed him was... Shocking. And... disgusting, for him. What Set offered had nothing to do with his distorted view of the &quot;greater good&quot;. Despite that, he went along with the &quot;program&quot; of course. So, my char sought out a very very poor family that had the purest, most honest and most honorable possible morals with a young daughter with those ideals, and posing himself as a rich noble seeking a honourable wife, I proposed to her father, even spousing the same high morals as his - claiming that the different social position (noble vs commoner) was far less important than morals, and that I needed a honorable wife more than a noble one. So, I married her and... Did things to her. Things only a setite would do.<br /> <br /> But, despite that, she kept her honor, she kept her morals, she did not fell into the same darkness as I. A mortal girl.<br /> <br /> That opened my eyes. That frail girl resisted the corruption while I, the &quot;great king&quot;, did not. And never had.<br /> <br /> Afterwards, I could no longer swallow Set's BS. After the fall of the Second City, I got away from &quot;dear father&quot;, eventually finding the Children of Osiris, and I joined.<br /> <br /> After a time thou, I ended up renegating them and I became a Hermit. I joined Saulot on his task to find Golconda, and I became an unsuferable buddhist niihist monk basically lol. I renegated the Children because I saw them as merely searching for vengeance, and I denounced them for that, but left on friendly terms, simply saying that their path was misguided.<br /> <br /> My dear wife became my kindred child, of course, before I joined the CoO. Eventually, Set captured her, trying to affect me. And he eventually managed to indoctrinate her. I had her being hidden away by Nosferati herself, but Set went to the point of waging war against the Nosferatu just to get her.<br /> <br /> When I learned of this, I went straight to see my &quot;father&quot;. I demanded to see her, but now she was a full Setite. And she said &quot;my love, just accept our father, and we can be togheter forever, freed of all the weight of morality&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, I came closer, hugged her, and gave her our last kiss... Because after kissing her for a few moments, crying while I did it, I used my forked serpentis tongue, and ripped her head off... Finally setting my beloved child-wife free, my greatest sin, and correcting my biggest mistake. And, at the same time, cutting my last connection to the world.<br /> <br /> Now I was finally free. Set was furious, as I became the first to truly resist him, and because he knew there was nothing else he could do. So I asked a private audience, and in it we made a bet for the soul of the world; I would bring hope and redemption for those looking for it, while he would keep corrupting, and we would see who could truly win. He asked why would he allow that, and I said &quot;you can only corrupt that which is pure&quot;.<br /> <br /> And he left me go, and the FoS never bothered me again, nor did I bother them.<br /> <br /> And I became the most pacifist monk ever; I even went absolutely out of the Jihad for good, and living or dying was indifferent for me.<br /> <br /> Edit: many many thousands of years later, I became a councelor on both Rome and Carthage. I would give wisdom bits to anyone willing to hear; sadly, only about Catherine the (not-yet) Wise was willing to hear. Yep, she was my pupil, but I was &quot;free&quot; from passions - I liked her, but had no attachments to anyone.<br /> <br /> That was the greatest game I ever played, and a great insight into elder mentality. My character became someone completely detached from everything - Humanity 10 on paper, but inhuman in reality, since he wouldnt hurt a fly, but at the same time felt... Nothing. He was just a poor little old man, that was truly dead inside. I became &quot;the librarian&quot;, dedicating myself to the study of thaumaturgy, but not for the sake of power, but simply for the knowledge by the knowledge, and I would expand the rest of eternity just tending in an absolutely and unpassionate way to my books and my studies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24689,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","user":"Kakost","date":". 07-04-2022, 04:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}